rer\">Every hurricane season\x3C/a> brings uncertainty.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cp>Not every supply chain experiences it the same way.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cp>Long before a tropical system appears on the weather map, decisions about ports, terminals, rail connections, and inland routing have already determined how resilient a shipment will be when severe weather arrives.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cp>As the Atlantic hurricane season reaches its traditional peak during late summer and early fall, shippers face the added challenge of preparing for weather disruptions while freight volumes begin climbing ahead of the holiday shipping season.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cp>The forecast matters.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cp>But the infrastructure supporting your freight often matters even more.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Ch2>\x3Cstrong>Not Every Port Faces the Same Risks\x3C/strong>\x3C/h2>\n\n\x3Cp>When people think about hurricanes, they often assume every East Coast or Gulf Coast port faces the same challenges.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cp>The reality is much more nuanced.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cp>Geography plays a significant role in how quickly a port can recover and how severely operations may be affected.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cp>Consider a few examples:\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cdiv class=\"article-table\">\n\x3Cdiv class=\"article-table-row article-table-row--head\">\n\x3Cdiv class=\"article-table-cell\">Port\x3C/div>\n\x3Cdiv class=\"article-table-cell\">Infrastructure Considerations\x3C/div>\n\x3C/div>\n\x3Cdiv class=\"article-table-row\">\n\x3Cdiv class=\"article-table-cell\" data-label=\"Port\">Port of Savannah\x3C/div>\n\x3Cdiv class=\"article-table-cell\" data-label=\"Infrastructure Considerations\">Located approximately 18 miles inland along the Savannah River, reducing direct storm surge exposure while remaining vulnerable to river flooding and navigation delays following heavy rainfall.\x3C/div>\n\x3C/div>\n\x3Cdiv class=\"article-table-row\">\n\x3Cdiv class=\"article-table-cell\" data-label=\"Port\">Port of Charleston\x3C/div>\n\x3Cdiv class=\"article-table-cell\" data-label=\"Infrastructure Considerations\">Marine terminals sit much closer to the Atlantic Ocean, increasing exposure to storm surge, coastal flooding, and temporary closure of surrounding road networks.\x3C/div>\n\x3C/div>\n\x3Cdiv class=\"article-table-row\">\n\x3Cdiv class=\"article-table-cell\" data-label=\"Port\">Port Houston\x3C/div>\n\x3Cdiv class=\"article-table-cell\" data-label=\"Infrastructure Considerations\">Located well inland through the Houston Ship Channel, avoiding direct ocean surge but remaining susceptible to extensive regional flooding that can disrupt trucking, rail, and warehouse operations.\x3C/div>\n\x3C/div>\n\x3C/div>\n\n\x3Cp>Each location presents different strengths and different risks.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cp>Understanding those differences allows shippers to make routing decisions that extend beyond transit time and transportation cost.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Ch2>\x3Cstrong>Infrastructure Determines Recovery Speed\x3C/strong>\x3C/h2>\n\n\x3Cp>One of the least discussed aspects of hurricane planning is how cargo moves after it leaves the vessel.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cp>This is where rail infrastructure becomes especially important.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cp>Many importers assume rail is rail.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cp>Operationally, that's not the case.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Ch3>\x3Cstrong>On-Dock Rail\x3C/strong>\x3C/h3>\n\n\x3Cp>When rail tracks are located inside the marine terminal:\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cul>\n\x3Cli>Containers are transferred directly from the ship to the train.\x3C/li>\n\x3Cli>Cargo remains inside the secure terminal.\x3C/li>\n\x3Cli>Internal terminal equipment performs the transfer.\x3C/li>\n\x3Cli>Public roads, local traffic, and outside drayage are largely removed from the equation.\x3C/li>\n\x3C/ul>\n\n\x3Cp>If terminal operations resume, cargo can often continue moving almost immediately.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Ch3>\x3Cstrong>Off-Dock Rail\x3C/strong>\x3C/h3>\n\n\x3Cp>When rail facilities are located outside the port:\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cul>\n\x3Cli>Containers must first leave the terminal.\x3C/li>\n\x3Cli>Freight depends on chassis availability.\x3C/li>\n\x3Cli>Local drayage providers become essential.\x3C/li>\n\x3Cli>Public roads must remain accessible.\x3C/li>\n\x3Cli>Flooding, debris, or power outages can interrupt every step.\x3C/li>\n\x3C/ul>\n\n\x3Cp>Even if the marine terminal itself escapes major damage, freight can still sit idle waiting for the surrounding transportation network to recover.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cp>Every additional handoff creates another opportunity for delays.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Ch2>\x3Cstrong>Small Infrastructure Decisions Become Big Storm Problems\x3C/strong>\x3C/h2>\n\n\x3Cp>Major weather events rarely disrupt just one part of the supply chain.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cp>Instead, they create a series of cascading operational challenges.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cp>During hurricane season, common disruptions include:\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cul>\n\x3Cli>Temporary port closures before landfall.\x3C/li>\n\x3Cli>Vessel schedule adjustments.\x3C/li>\n\x3Cli>Restricted gate operations.\x3C/li>\n\x3Cli>Flooded access roads.\x3C/li>\n\x3Cli>Reduced chassis availability.\x3C/li>\n\x3Cli>Drayage shortages.\x3C/li>\n\x3Cli>Rail service interruptions.\x3C/li>\n\x3Cli>Power outages affecting terminal operations.\x3C/li>\n\x3Cli>Demurrage and detention charges as free time expires.\x3C/li>\n\x3C/ul>\n\n\x3Cp>The faster freight can move through each handoff, the faster normal operations can resume once conditions improve.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Ch2>\x3Cstrong>Prepare Before the Forecast Changes\x3C/strong>\x3C/h2>\n\n\x3Cp>Waiting until a storm enters the forecast often limits your available options.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cp>Instead, shippers should review contingency plans before the busiest weeks of hurricane season arrive.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cp>A proactive review might include:\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cul>\n\x3Cli>Evaluating alternative ports if weather threatens your primary gateway.\x3C/li>\n\x3Cli>Understanding whether your intermodal routing relies on on-dock or off-dock rail.\x3C/li>\n\x3Cli>Confirming warehouse capacity outside immediate flood zones.\x3C/li>\n\x3Cli>Reviewing cargo insurance to understand weather-related coverage.\x3C/li>\n\x3Cli>Maintaining relationships with regional transportation providers for additional flexibility.\x3C/li>\n\x3Cli>Identifying critical shipments that should move ahead of potential storm activity.\x3C/li>\n\x3C/ul>\n\n\x3Cp>These conversations are easier — and far less expensive — before a storm begins affecting operations.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cp>When a hurricane develops, everyone watches the forecast. Experienced logistics professionals are already looking at the infrastructure. The way a port is designed often determines how quickly freight starts moving again once the weather clears.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Ch2>\x3Cstrong>Build Resilience Into Your Supply Chain\x3C/strong>\x3C/h2>\n\n\x3Cp>No logistics provider can prevent severe weather.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cp>What good planning can do is reduce unnecessary disruption.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cp>Choosing the right gateway, understanding terminal infrastructure, and planning transportation alternatives before hurricane season peaks all contribute to a more resilient supply chain.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cp>At Blue Ridge International, we help customers evaluate transportation strategies that consider more than rates and transit times. We examine the operational realities behind every routing decision so that, when conditions change, your supply chain is prepared to adapt.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cp>Because when hurricane season reaches its busiest weeks, resilience begins long before the first storm forms.\x3C/p>\n\n\x3Cp>\x3Ca href=\"/contact\">Contact Blue Ridge International\x3C/a> to discuss routing strategies, port selection, and contingency planning that help keep your freight moving during hurricane season.\x3C/p>\n"},ssr:!0}],lastMatchId:"␀news_␀$slug␀news␀hurricane-port-infrastructure"})($R["tsr"]);$_TSR.e();document.currentScript.remove()</script><script type="module" async="">import("/assets/main-ZZ1CkOAP.js")</script></body></html>